#
# Sweep table for launch_sweep.py. One run per row, top to bottom.
#
# The first non-comment line is the header: each column names a key that the script rewrites
# in the profile before that run. A dotted path names the exact place; a bare name also works
# when it is unique in the file. Not limited to the columns below:
#
#   benchmark_options   num_prompts, max_concurrency, num_warmups,
#                       random_input_len, random_output_len
#   timeouts            timeouts.workload, timeouts.metrics_available, timeouts.quiesce
#   serving             model, tensor_parallel
#
# Add a column and every row must supply a value for it. Blank lines and #-comments are
# ignored. An optional `name` column overrides the generated report filename.
#
# Rows 1-11 ramp max_concurrency to locate the knee. Rows 12-13 stop climbing and grow the
# sample instead: past saturation a higher concurrency measures queueing rather than serving,
# and far enough past it the slowest requests exceed benchmark_serving.py's failure-rate gate
# and the row fails outright.
#
# Rows 12-13 therefore soak one operating point. Change their max_concurrency to soak a
# different one -- the concurrency worth holding is the one where throughput plateaus, which
# the ramp above is what identifies.
#
# num_warmups is held constant on rows 12-13 rather than scaled with num_prompts: roughly two
# concurrency-widths is enough to fill the pipeline, and keeping it fixed makes those two rows
# differ only in sample length.
#
# timeouts.workload carries deliberate headroom over each row's expected duration, estimated as
# (num_prompts + num_warmups) / observed requests-per-second. A row killed by its timeout
# produces nothing at all, so over-allowing costs only the wait when something goes wrong.

max_concurrency   num_prompts   num_warmups   timeouts.workload
1                 64            2             1800
4                 64            8             1800
8                 64            16            1800
16                128           32            1800
32                256           64            1800
64                512           128           1800
128               1024          256           2400
256               2048          512           3000
512               4096          1024          3600
1024              8192          2048          5400
2048              16384         4096          7200
2048              20480         4096          7200
2048              24576         4096          7200
